sn46.48:1.3 | S: A mendicant with good friends can expect to develop and cultivate the seven awakening factors. | T: When a bhikkhu has a good friend, it is to be expected that he will develop and cultivate the seven factors of enlightenment.
sn46.48:1.4 | S: And how does a mendicant with good friends develop and cultivate the seven awakening factors? | T: “And how does a bhikkhu who has a good friend develop and cultivate the seven factors of enlightenment?
sn46.48:1.5 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops the awakening factors of mindfulness, investigation of principles, energy, rapture, tranquility, immersion, | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu develops the enlightenment factor of mindfulness … he develops the enlightenment factor of equanimity,
sn46.48:1.6 | S: and equanimity, which rely on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripen as letting go. | T: which is based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, maturing in release.
sn46.48:1.7 | S: That’s how a mendicant with good friends develops and cultivates the seven awakening factors.” | T: It is in this way that a bhikkhu who has a good friend develops and cultivates the seven factors of enlightenment.”
sn46.48:1.8 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

