## sn46.53
sn46.53:1.1 | S: Then several mendicants robed up in the morning and, taking their bowls and robes, entered Sāvatthī for alms. | T: Then, in the morning, a number of bhikkhus dressed and, taking their bowls and robes, entered Savatthī for alms …
sn46.53:1.2 | S: (The same as the previous discourse.) | T: as in §52 down to: …
sn46.53:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, when wanderers of other religions say this, you should say to them: | T: The Blessed One said: “Bhikkhus, when wanderers of other sects speak thus, they should be asked:
sn46.53:2.2 | S: ‘Reverends, which awakening factors should not be developed when the mind is sluggish? And which awakening factors should be developed at that time? | T: ‘Friends, when the mind becomes sluggish, which factors of enlightenment is it untimely to develop on that occasion, and which factors of enlightenment is it timely to develop on that occasion?
sn46.53:2.3 | S: Which awakening factors should not be developed when the mind is restless? And which awakening factors should be developed at that time?’ | T: Then, friends, when the mind becomes excited, which factors of enlightenment is it untimely to develop on that occasion, and which factors of enlightenment is it timely to develop on that occasion?’
sn46.53:2.4 | S: Questioned like this, the wanderers of other religions would be stumped, and, in addition, would get frustrated. | T: Being asked thus, those wanderers would not be able to reply and, further, they would meet with vexation.
sn46.53:2.5 | S: Why is that? | T: For what reason?
sn46.53:2.6 | S: Because they’re out of their element. | T: Because that would not be within their domain.
sn46.53:3.1 | S: I don’t see anyone in this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans—who could provide a satisfying answer to these questions except the Realized One or his disciple or someone who has heard it from them. | T: I do not see anyone, bhikkhus, in this world with its devas, Mara, and Brahma, in this generation with its ascetics and brahmins, its devas and humans, who could satisfy the mind with an answer to these questions except the Tathagata or a disciple of the Tathagata or one who has heard it from them.
sn46.53:4.1 | S: When the mind is sluggish, it’s the wrong time to develop the awakening factors of tranquility, immersion, and equanimity. | T: i. The sluggish mind: untimely “On an occasion, bhikkhus, when the mind becomes sluggish, it is untimely to develop the enlightenment factor of tranquillity, the enlightenment factor of concentration, and the enlightenment factor of equanimity.
sn46.53:4.2 | S: Why is that? | T: For what reason?
sn46.53:4.3 | S: Because it’s hard to stimulate a sluggish mind with these things. | T: Because the mind is sluggish, bhikkhus, and it is difficult to arouse it with those things.
sn46.53:5.1 | S: Suppose someone wanted to make a small fire flare up. | T: “Suppose, bhikkhus, a man wants to make a small fire flare up.
sn46.53:5.2 | S: If they toss wet grass, cow-dung, and timber on it, spray it with water, and scatter dirt on it, | T: If he throws wet grass, wet cowdung, and wet timber into it, sprays it with water, and scatters soil over it,
sn46.53:5.3 | S: could they make it flare up?” | T: would he be able to make that small fire flare up?”
sn46.53:5.4 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, venerable sir.”
sn46.53:6.1 | S: “In the same way, when the mind is sluggish, it’s the wrong time to develop the awakening factors of tranquility, immersion, and equanimity. | T: “So too, bhikkhus, on an occasion when the mind becomes sluggish, it is untimely to develop the enlightenment factor of tranquillity, the enlightenment factor of concentration, and the enlightenment factor of equanimity.
sn46.53:6.2 | S: Why is that? | T: For what reason?
sn46.53:6.3 | S: Because it’s hard to stimulate a sluggish mind with these things. | T: Because the mind is sluggish, bhikkhus, and it is difficult to arouse it with those things.
sn46.53:7.1 | S: When the mind is sluggish, it’s the right time to develop the awakening factors of investigation of principles, energy, and rapture. | T: ii. The sluggish mind: timely “On an occasion, bhikkhus, when the mind becomes sluggish, it is timely to develop the enlightenment factor of discrimination of states, the enlightenment factor of energy, and the enlightenment factor of rapture.
sn46.53:7.2 | S: Why is that? | T: For what reason?
sn46.53:7.3 | S: Because it’s easy to stimulate a sluggish mind with these things. | T: Because the mind is sluggish, bhikkhus, and it is easy to arouse it with those things.
sn46.53:8.1 | S: Suppose someone wanted to make a small fire flare up. | T: “Suppose, bhikkhus, a man wants to make a small fire flare up.
sn46.53:8.2 | S: If they toss dry grass, cow-dung, and timber on it, blow on it, and don’t scatter dirt on it, | T: If he throws dry grass, dry cowdung, and dry timber into it, blows on it, and does not scatter soil over it,
sn46.53:8.3 | S: could they make it flare up?” | T: would he be able to make that small fire flare up?”

